## Yes – you can draw contour lines **directly from a `raster-dem` source at runtime**  
using the **`maplibre-contour`** library. The library runs a Web-Worker that reads the DEM tiles, extracts elevation isolines, and serves them to MapLibre through a custom protocol URL. No pre-generated vector contour tileset is required.

Below is a complete, step-by-step guide that works with the current MapLibre GL JS (≥ v5.6) and `maplibre-contour` (≥ v0.2). Adjust the URLs, DEM encoding, and styling to fit your own data.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| What you need | Why |
|---------------|-----|
| **MapLibre GL JS** (v5.6+) | Provides the `raster-dem` source type, `["elevation"]` expression, and the `setTerrain`/`setSky` APIs. |
| **DEM tiles** (WebP, PNG, or JPEG) | The source of raw elevation values. Must be a *raster-dem* source, not a regular raster image. |
| **DEM encoding** (`mapbox`, `terrarium`, `raw`) | Tells the library how to decode the pixel values into meters. Must match the source’s `encoding` property. |
| **`maplibre-contour`** npm package (or a bundled copy) | Generates the contour vectors on the fly. |
| **A modern browser** (Web-Worker + OffscreenCanvas support) | Required for the worker that does the heavy lifting. |

> **Tip:** If you are already using `map.setTerrain({source: 'my-dem'})` you can reuse the same DEM source for contours – just add the extra vector source that `maplibre-contour` creates.

---

## 2. Install / import the library

### Using a bundler (Webpack, Vite, etc.)

```bash
npm install maplibre-gl maplibre-contour
```

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';
```

### Using a plain `<script>` tag

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
<script>
  const { Map } = maplibregl;          // MapLibre global
  const { DemSource } = mlcontour;    // maplibre-contour global
</script>
```

> **Important:** The library ships both an ES-module build (`mlcontour`) and a UMD build (`mlcontour.min.js`). Use the one that matches your environment.

---

## 3. Create the DEM source (the same one you’ll use for terrain)

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the raster-dem source (you can also add it via a style JSON)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp', // <-- your DEM tiles
  tileSize: 256,
  // Choose the correct encoding for your DEM tiles:
  //   'mapbox'   – Mapbox terrain-rgb (0-255 per channel)
  //   'terrarium' – Terrarium (R*256 + G + B/256 – 32768)
  //   'raw'      – 16-bit signed integer stored in the red channel
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  maxzoom: 12
});
```

> **Why `encoding` matters:** The worker must decode the pixel values exactly the same way the source does. If you use Mapbox’s `terrain-rgb`, set `encoding: 'mapbox'`; for Terrarium tiles, use `'terrarium'`.

---

## 4. Initialise the contour generator

```js
// 2️⃣ Create a DemSource that knows how to read the same DEM tiles
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // Same URL pattern as the raster-dem source above
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
  // Must match the source’s encoding
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // The highest zoom level you expect to request contours for
  maxzoom: 12,
  // Whether to run the worker in a separate thread (recommended)
  worker: true
});

// 3️⃣ Register the worker with the MapLibre instance
demSource.setupMaplibre(map);
```

*`setupMaplibre(map)`* does two things:

1. Registers a custom protocol handler (`mlcontour://`) that MapLibre can request as a vector source.
2. Starts the Web-Worker (or a shared worker) that will decode DEM tiles and generate isolines.

---

## 5. Add a **virtual vector source** that points to the contour protocol

```js
// 4️⃣ Add a vector source that will be filled by the contour worker
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is built by the DemSource; you can pass options here:
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // ── Contour generation options ────────────────────────
      // Elevation intervals (in metres) for each zoom level.
      // The library will generate *minor* and *major* contours.
      thresholds: {
        // zoom: [minorInterval, majorInterval]
        11: [200, 1000],
        12: [100, 500],
        13: [50, 250],
        14: [25, 100],
        15: [10, 50]
      },
      // Property names that will appear on each feature
      elevationKey: 'ele',   // numeric elevation (meters)
      levelKey: 'level',     // 0 = minor, 1 = major
      // Optional: give the layer a stable name (used for caching)
      contourLayer: 'contours'
    })
  ],
  // The worker can generate tiles up to this zoom; keep it a bit higher
  // than the DEM source so you can zoom in a little before the contours stop.
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

### What the options mean

| Option | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `thresholds` | A map of **zoom → [minor, major]** intervals (in metres). The library will emit two sets of lines per tile: *minor* (level 0) and *major* (level 1). |
| `elevationKey` | Property name that stores the exact elevation of the line (e.g., `ele: 1200`). Useful for labeling. |
| `levelKey` | Property name that tells you whether the line is a major contour (`1`) or a minor one (`0`). |
| `contourLayer` | An arbitrary string used for internal caching; keep it stable across page loads if you want the worker to reuse already-computed tiles. |
| `worker` (when creating `DemSource`) | `true` runs the heavy work in a separate thread, keeping the UI smooth. Set to `false` only for debugging. |

---

## 6. Add a **line layer** to draw the contours

```js
// 5️⃣ Draw the contour lines
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',   // the name we gave in `contourLayer`
  layout: {
    // Optional: make the lines smoother at high zoom
    'line-cap': 'round',
    'line-join': 'round'
  },
  paint: {
    // Colour based on major/minor – you can also use a data-driven style.
    'line-color': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, // major contour
      'rgba(0,0,0,0.8)', // darker
      'rgba(0,0,0,0.4)'  // minor contour
    ],
    // Thicker lines for majors
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1,
      2.0,   // major
      0.8    // minor
    ]
  }
});
```

### Optional: label the major contours

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1], // only major lines
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false,
    'text-offset': [0, 0.5]
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

---

## 7. (Optional) Combine with 3-D terrain & sky

If you already have a terrain mesh, you can keep it on while drawing contours:

```js
// Enable 3-D terrain (same DEM source)
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.2 });

// Add a sky for a realistic horizon
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#88ccee',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-color': '#cceeff',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

Contours will automatically drape over the terrain because they are drawn from the same DEM data.

---

## 8. Performance & best-practice notes

| Concern | Recommendation |
|---------|----------------|
| **Worker memory** | The worker caches DEM tiles it has already decoded. Keep `maxzoom` modest (≤ 12-13) unless you truly need high-resolution contours. |
| **Tile over-fetch** | The contour protocol only requests DEM tiles that intersect the current viewport at the requested zoom. No extra network traffic beyond what your terrain already loads. |
| **Styling complexity** | Keep the line style simple (solid colour, modest width). Adding a heavy `line-gradient` or many label layers can degrade FPS on low-end devices. |
| **Zoom-dependent thresholds** | Use coarser intervals at low zoom (e.g., 200 m) and finer intervals at high zoom (e.g., 10 m). This reduces the number of generated lines and keeps the map readable. |
| **Browser support** | The library falls back to the main thread if Web-Workers are unavailable, but you’ll see UI jank. Test on the browsers you target. |
| **Cache busting** | If you change the DEM URL or its encoding, recreate the `DemSource` (or call `demSource.clearCache()`) so the worker discards stale tiles. |

---

