Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that turns the blank page-background you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the code works with the current stable version of **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.6** (the version that introduced the `sky` object).

---

## 1. Why the blank background appears

* `map.setTerrain()` creates a 3-D mesh, but **nothing draws the “sky”**.  
* In MapLibre the sky is **not a layer** – it is a **root-level `sky` object** (or the equivalent `map.setSky()` call).  
* The old Mapbox method `map.setFog()` does **not exist** in MapLibre, so calling it does nothing and you end up with the default HTML page background (usually white or whatever your `<body>` colour is).

---

## 2. What you need

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **A `raster-dem` source** (already present) | Provides the elevation data that makes the terrain mesh. |
| **`map.setTerrain({ source: "...", exaggeration: … })`** | Turns the DEM into a 3-D surface. |
| **A pitched camera** (`pitch: 60-80`) | Makes the terrain visible and also enables the sky rendering. |
| **A `sky` object** (or `map.setSky()`) | Draws the actual sky and fog/haze. |
| **A background layer that is either transparent or placed **below** the sky** | Prevents the background colour from covering the sky. |

---

## 3. Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – terrain + sky + fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style you like)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // or your own style URL
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // lon, lat
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,          // 👈 look up at the horizon
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true    // needed for smooth terrain shading
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Add the DEM source (only once)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // a raster-dem source – replace the URL with your own DEM tileset
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json', // <-- your DEM TileJSON
      tileSize: 256,
      // optional: encoding: 'terrarium' | 'mapbox' (default is mapbox)
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Enable 3-D terrain
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.setTerrain({
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      exaggeration: 1.2   // tweak if you want more “relief”
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Add the sky (this is the *only* thing that draws
    //     the colour above the horizon)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.setSky({
      // overall sky colour – a light blue works well for a clear day
      'sky-color': '#87CEEB',

      // colour of the horizon line (where sky meets ground)
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',

      // how quickly the sky colour blends into the horizon colour
      // 0 = abrupt line, 1 = very smooth transition
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

      // fog colour – this is the “haze” that appears near the ground
      // It must be a colour that works with the terrain; a soft blue works.
      'fog-color': '#cce0ff',

      // how much the fog blends with the horizon colour
      // 0 = no fog at the horizon, 1 = fog completely covers the horizon
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,

      // how much the fog blends with the ground (terrain)
      // 0 = fog only in the sky, 1 = fog reaches all the way to the ground
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  (Optional) Make sure the background layer does not hide the sky
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // If your style has a background layer with a solid colour,
    // set its opacity to 0 or remove it.
    const bg = map.getLayer('background');
    if (bg) {
      map.setPaintProperty('background', 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 6️⃣  Add any other layers you need (hillshade, labels, etc.)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Example: a simple hillshade layer to give the terrain some relief
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
        'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional',
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Step | Code | Effect |
|------|------|--------|
| **1️⃣** | `new maplibregl.Map({ pitch: 70 })` | Tilts the camera so the horizon is visible. |
| **2️⃣** | `addSource('terrain-dem', {type:'raster-dem', …})` | Supplies the elevation tiles. |
| **3️⃣** | `map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem', exaggeration:1.2})` | Builds the 3-D mesh. |
| **4️⃣** | `map.setSky({ … })` | Draws the sky, horizon line, and fog (haze). |
| **5️⃣** | `setPaintProperty('background', …)` | Guarantees the HTML page background does not cover the sky. |
| **6️⃣** | `addLayer({type:'hillshade', …})` | (Optional) adds a soft hillshade so the terrain looks natural. |

---

## 4. Tweaking the sky & fog

| Property | Typical range | Visual effect |
|----------|---------------|---------------|
| `sky-color` | any CSS colour | Overall colour of the sky. Light blues for clear days, deeper blues for twilight, or even a sunset orange. |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | `0 … 1` | Larger values give a smoother gradient from sky to horizon. |
| `horizon-color` | any CSS colour | Colour of the thin line where sky meets ground. Often white or a very light version of `sky-color`. |
| `fog-color` | any CSS colour (usually a muted blue/gray) | Colour of the haze that appears near the ground. |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | `0 … 1` | Controls how much fog is visible right at the horizon. |
| `fog-ground-blend` | `0 … 1` | Controls how far the fog reaches down the terrain. Values around `0.4-0.6` give a natural “atmospheric perspective”. |

**Tip:** If you want a “sunset” look, set `sky-color` to a warm orange (`#ffb347`), `horizon-color` to a pale pink (`#ffd1dc`), and keep the fog a soft gray (`#c0c0c0`). The fog will still give depth to the mountains.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Sky still missing** | `map.setSky()` called **before** the map has loaded, or before the terrain source is added. | Call `map.setSky()` **inside** the `map.on('load', …)` handler (as shown). |
| **Background colour still shows** | The style contains a `background` layer with `background-color` set to an opaque value. | Either remove that layer or set its opacity to `0` (`rgba(0,0,0,0)`). |
| **Fog does not appear** | Terrain is not enabled **or** the map is at `pitch: 0`. | Ensure `map.setTerrain()` is active **and** the camera pitch is > 0 (e.g., 45°+). |
| **`setSky` is undefined** | Using a MapLibre version older than 5.5. | Upgrade to at least **5.5** (5.6+ for `color-relief`, but sky works from 5.5). |
| **Horizon line is a hard black line** | `sky-horizon-blend` left at the default `0`. | Increase it to `0.3-0.6`. |
| **Fog looks too strong / too weak** | `horizon-fog-blend` or `fog-ground-blend` values are extreme. | Adjust those two properties until the haze looks natural. |

---

## 6. Full API reference (quick cheat-sheet)

```js
map.setSky({
  'sky-color':            string, // CSS colour
  'sky-horizon-blend':    number, // 0-1
  'horizon-color':        string, // CSS colour
  'horizon-fog-blend':    number, // 0-1
  'fog-color':            string, // CSS colour
  'fog-ground-blend':     number  // 0-1
});
```

*Only these six paint-style properties are recognised.*  
Any other property (e.g., `sky-type`, `sky-atmosphere-sun`) will cause a **validation error** and the style will not load.

---

## 7. Putting it all together in a reusable function

If you enable terrain in many maps, you can wrap the sky setup in a helper:

```js
function enableTerrainAndSky(map, demUrl, options = {}) {
  const {
    exaggeration = 1,
    skyColor = '#87CEEB',
    horizonColor = '#ffffff',
    fogColor = '#cce0ff',
    skyHorizonBlend = 0.5,
    horizonFogBlend = 0.4,
    fogGroundBlend = 0.5,
    pitch = 70
  } = options;

  // 1️⃣ DEM source
  map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    url: demUrl,
    tileSize: 256
  });

  // 2️⃣ Terrain mesh
  map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration });

  // 3️⃣ Camera pitch (if you want to enforce it)
  map.easeTo({ pitch });

  // 4️⃣ Sky & fog
  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': skyColor,
    'sky-horizon-blend': skyHorizonBlend,
    'horizon-color': horizonColor,
    'horizon-fog-blend': horizonFogBlend,
    'fog-color': fogColor,
    'fog-ground-blend': fogGroundBlend
  });

  // 5️⃣ Make background transparent (optional)
  const bg = map.getLayer('background');
  if (bg) map.setPaintProperty('background', 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
}
```

Usage:

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  enableTerrainAndSky(map, 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json', {
    exaggeration: 1.3,
    skyColor: '#a0c8f0',
    fogColor: '#d0e6ff',
    pitch: 75
  });
});
```

---

## 8. TL;DR – the minimal checklist

1. **Add a `raster-dem` source** (your DEM tiles).  
2. **Call `map.setTerrain({source: 'your-dem', exaggeration

Below is a short, step-by-step recipe for putting a **pre-rendered shaded-relief basemap (JPEG/PNG tiles)** behind all of your vector data (roads, buildings, labels, etc.) in a MapLibre GL JS map.

| What you have | What you need to do |
|---------------|---------------------|
| A tile server that returns JPEG (or PNG) tiles, e.g. `https://mytiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg` | Add a **raster source** that points at those tiles. |
| Vector sources/layers already in the style (or you will add them later) | Add a **raster layer** that uses the raster source **before** any other layers, then keep adding your vector layers on top (or move them above the raster layer). |

---

## 1. Add the raster source

```js
// Assuming you already have a `map` instance:
map.addSource('shaded-relief-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  // The URL template that returns the JPEG tiles.
  // You can also use a TileJSON URL if you have one.
  tiles: [
    'https://mytiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
  ],
  // Optional but recommended – tells the renderer the tile size.
  tileSize: 256,          // most raster tiles are 256 px; use 512 if that’s what you serve
  // If your tiles are in a different projection (unlikely for web maps) you would need
  // a custom source type; for standard Web Mercator just leave it as is.
  maxzoom: 14,            // set to the highest zoom you actually serve
  attribution: '© My Company'   // optional, for the map UI
});
```

> **Why `type: 'raster'`?**  
> A pre-rendered image tile is *not* a DEM. It must be treated as a raster source, not a `raster-dem`. The `raster-dem` type is only for elevation data that MapLibre can decode into a mesh.

---

## 2. Add the raster layer

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'shaded-relief-basemap',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'shaded-relief-basemap',
  // Paint properties are optional; they let you tweak opacity, hue, etc.
  paint: {
    // Keep the basemap fully opaque (1) or lower it if you want the vector data to “show through”
    'raster-opacity': 1.0,
    // If you need to brighten/darken the basemap you can use a color matrix:
    // 'raster-color': ['rgba', 255, 255, 255, 0.9]   // example: slight brightening
  }
});
```

### Placement matters

MapLibre draws layers **in the order they appear in the style** (first = bottom).  
If you add the raster layer *after* you have already added your vector layers, it will appear **on top** of them, covering the labels.

#### Two ways to guarantee the correct order

1. **Add the raster layer first**, then add all vector layers later (the simplest approach).  
2. **Add the raster layer at any time, then move it to the bottom**:

   ```js
   // Move the raster layer to be the first layer in the style
   const firstLayerId = map.getStyle().layers[0].id; // whatever is currently first
   map.moveLayer('shaded-relief-basemap', firstLayerId);
   // Or, to be absolutely sure it’s the very bottom:
   map.moveLayer('shaded-relief-basemap');
   ```

   `map.moveLayer(layerId, beforeId?)` with no `beforeId` moves the layer to the top of the stack; to move it to the bottom you give the *current* first layer’s id as the `beforeId`.

---

## 3. (Optional) Add a background layer for “no-data” areas

If your raster tiles don’t cover the whole world (e.g., they stop at zoom 12), you may want a background colour underneath so you don’t see a black or transparent void.

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'basemap-background',
  type: 'background',
  paint: {
    'background-color': '#e0e0e0'   // light gray, or any colour you like
  }
}, 'shaded-relief-basemap'); // insert it *below* the raster layer
```

---

## 4. Add your vector data (roads, buildings, labels, …)

```js
// Example: a vector source with roads
map.addSource('roads', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'mapbox://myaccount.myroads'   // or any other vector source URL
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'road-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'roads',
  'source-layer': 'road',
  paint: {
    'line-color': '#ffffff',
    'line-width': 2
  }
});

// Example: a label layer that should sit on top of everything
map.addLayer({
  id: 'place-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'places',
  'source-layer': 'place',
  layout: {
    'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#000000'
  }
});
```

Because the raster layer is at the bottom, all of the above vector layers will automatically render on top.

---

## 5. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Shaded-relief basemap + vector labels</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},   // we’ll add sources programmatically
      layers: []     // same for layers
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,          // optional – tilt to see the relief in 3-D
    bearing: -17
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Raster source + layer (the shaded-relief basemap)
    map.addSource('shaded-relief-basemap', {
      type: 'raster',
      tiles: ['https://mytiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 14,
      attribution: '© My Company'
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'shaded-relief-basemap',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'shaded-relief-basemap',
      paint: { 'raster-opacity': 1 }
    });

    // 2️⃣ (Optional) background for uncovered areas
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'basemap-background',
      type: 'background',
      paint: { 'background-color': '#e0e0e0' }
    }, 'shaded-relief-basemap'); // insert below raster

    // 3️⃣ Vector source + road layer
    map.addSource('roads', {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'https://mytiles.example.com/roads.json'   // replace with your source
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'road-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'roads',
      'source-layer': 'road',
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#fff',
        'line-width': 2
      }
    });

    // 4️⃣ Label layer (on top)
    map.addSource('places', {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'https://mytiles.example.com/places.json'
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'place-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'places',
      'source-layer': 'place',
      layout: {
        'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
        'text-size': 12,
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
      },
      paint: { 'text-color': '#000' }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace the URLs with the actual endpoints for your raster tiles and vector sources.*

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Labels disappear under the basemap** | Raster layer added *after* the label layer (or `moveLayer` placed it above). | Add raster first, or call `map.moveLayer('shaded-relief-basemap', 'place-labels')` to push it below the label layer. |
| **Basemap looks blurry or pixelated** | Tile size mismatch (`tileSize` set incorrectly) or using a low-resolution source at high zoom. | Verify the source actually serves 256 px (or 512 px) tiles at the zoom levels you request. |
| **White/black gaps where tiles are missing** | The raster source does not have tiles for the current zoom/extent. | Either provide tiles for those zoom levels, or add a background layer (see step 3) to fill the gaps. |
| **Map is completely gray/black** | The raster source URL is wrong, CORS blocked, or the server returns 404. | Open the tile URL directly in a browser to confirm it works, and ensure the server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |
| **Performance drops when panning** | Very large raster tiles (e.g., 1024 px) or many high-resolution layers stacked. | Keep `tileSize` at 256 px, limit the number of raster layers, and consider using `raster-resampling: 'linear'` if you need smoother scaling. |

---

## 7. When would you *instead* use a DEM-based hillshade?

If you ever want the relief to **react to map bearing** (light rotates with the map) or you need **dynamic exaggeration**, you would switch to a true DEM (`type: 'raster-dem'`) and a `hillshade` layer (or `color-relief`).  
But for a **static, pre-rendered JPEG basemap** you *don’t* need any of those – just treat it as a raster layer as shown above.

---

### TL;DR

1. **Add a `raster` source** that points at your JPEG tiles.  
2. **Add a `raster` layer** using that source.  
3. **Place that layer at the bottom** of the style (first layer, or move it before other layers).  
4. Add your vector sources/layers (roads, labels, etc.) **after** the raster layer, or move them above it.  
5. Optionally add a background layer for uncovered areas.

That’s all you need to get a beautiful shaded-relief basemap under your vector data with labels staying on top. Happy mapping!

### TL;DR  
`map.setFog()` is a **Mapbox-only** API.  
In MapLibre GL JS you get the same “haze-at-the-horizon” effect by adding a **sky object** (or calling `map.setSky()`) and configuring its fog-related properties.  
Fog only becomes visible when **3-D terrain** is enabled, otherwise you’ll just see the sky colour.

Below is a complete, copy-and-paste ready snippet that reproduces the classic Mapbox fog-look after you have migrated to MapLibre.

---

## 1. Make sure you have a `raster-dem` source and terrain turned on  

Fog is part of the sky rendering pipeline and is only applied when the map is drawing a 3-D terrain mesh.

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the DEM source (replace the URL with your own DEM tiles)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
  // optional, but recommended for better performance
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 12
});

// 2️⃣ Enable terrain (exaggeration can stay 1 unless you want it stronger)
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });
```

> **Why?**  
> Without terrain the sky is rendered, but the fog-related fields (`fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) are ignored, so you’ll just see a plain blue sky.

---

## 2. Add a sky object (or call `map.setSky`) with fog parameters  

You can either **declare the sky in the style JSON** or **set it at runtime**.  
Both approaches are equivalent; the runtime call is handy when you’re porting code that previously used `map.setFog()`.

### 2-a. Runtime call (closest to the old `setFog` API)

```js
// 3️⃣ Set the sky – this replaces the old map.setFog()
map.setSky({
  // ---- Sky colour (the colour of the “clear” part of the horizon)
  'sky-color': '#88ccee',          // light blue, adjust to taste

  // ---- Horizon colour (the line where sky meets terrain)
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',      // usually white or very pale

  // ---- Fog colour – this is the “haze” you were missing
  'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',          // a slightly desaturated blue

  // ---- How much the fog blends into the horizon (0 = no fog, 1 = full fog)
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,        // typical value; increase for thicker haze

  // ---- How much the fog blends into the ground (0 = no fog, 1 = full fog)
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,         // typical value; increase for deeper haze

  // ---- Optional: make the sky a little brighter near the zenith
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,        // blend between sky-color and horizon-color
  // 'atmosphere-blend': 0.2,      // newer property, can be left out
});
```

### 2-b. Declaring the sky in the style JSON (if you prefer a static style)

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": { /* … your sources … */ },
  "terrain": { "source": "terrain-dem", "exaggeration": 1 },
  "sky": {
    "sky-color": "#88ccee",
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",
    "fog-color": "#c0d8ff",
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.5,
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.5,
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5
  },
  "layers": [ /* … your layers … */ ]
}
```

> **Note:** The style-JSON version is useful if you ship a single style file; the runtime version is handy when you’re converting existing `map.setFog({ … })` calls.

---

## 3. (Optional) Tweak the camera so the fog is visible  

Fog becomes obvious when the camera is pitched back a bit, because you can see the horizon line.

```js
// Tilt the map so you can see the horizon and fog
map.easeTo({ pitch: 60, bearing: 0, duration: 1500 });
```

If you keep `pitch: 0` you’ll still get a sky colour, but the fog-gradient won’t be noticeable.

---

## 4. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Fog (sky) example</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 12
        },
        // add any raster or vector sources you need for basemap imagery here
      },
      terrain: { source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 },
      // sky is optional in the style – we’ll also set it at runtime below
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Enable terrain (already done via style, but you can also call setTerrain)
    // map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // 2️⃣ Add the sky/fog
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#88ccee',
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5
    });

    // 3️⃣ Tilt the camera so the fog is visible
    map.easeTo({ pitch: 60, duration: 1500 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## 5. Quick checklist when you migrate from `map.setFog`  

| ✅ What you had in Mapbox GL JS | ❌ What **doesn’t exist** in MapLibre | ✅ What you need to do in MapLibre |
|--------------------------------|--------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|
| `map.setFog({ "color": "#c0d8ff", "highColor": "#fff", "spaceColor": "#88ccee", "horizonBlend": 0.5 })` | `setFog` method, `fog` root object, `highColor`, `spaceColor` properties | Use `map.setSky()` (or a `sky` object) and set `fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`, `sky-color`, `horizon-color`. |
| Fog appeared even with a flat map | Fog works **only** when terrain is present | Add a `raster-dem` source and enable it with `map.setTerrain({ source: "...", exaggeration: 1 })`. |
| No need to change camera | Fog is invisible at `pitch: 0` | Tilt the map (`pitch > 0`) to see the gradient. |
| `map.setFog(null)` to disable | No `setFog(null)` | Call `map.setSky(null)` **or** remove the `sky` object from the style. |

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & how to fix them  

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No haze at all, just a solid blue sky** | Terrain not enabled or `terrain` source missing. | Add a `raster-dem` source and call `map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem' })`. |
| **Fog looks too thin / disappears quickly** | `horizon-fog-blend` or `fog-ground-blend` set to a low value (default is `0`). | Increase those values (e.g., `0.5` or `0.7`). |
| **Fog colour is wrong (too bright/dark)** | `fog-color` not matching your desired hue. | Choose a more muted colour (e.g., `#c0d8ff` for light blue, `#a0b8d0` for a greyer haze). |
| **Fog appears but the horizon line is missing** | `horizon-color` is the same as `sky-color` or fully transparent. | Set a contrasting `horizon-color` (usually white or very light). |
| **Console shows “unknown property: fog-color”** | Using an older MapLibre version (< 4.0) that didn’t yet implement the sky spec. | Upgrade to the latest MapLibre GL JS (≥ 4.4) or back-port the sky module. |
| **Fog works but the map is very slow** | Terrain mesh is heavy (high-resolution DEM, high `exaggeration`, many draped layers). | Reduce DEM maxzoom, lower `exaggeration`, and keep only essential layers on top of terrain. |

---

## 7. References (the “official” docs)

* **Sky & fog spec** – <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/sky/>
* **Terrain spec** – <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/terrain/>
* **MapLibre `Map` API** – `setTerrain`, `setSky`, `queryTerrainElevation` – <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/API/classes/Map/>
* **Example: sky-fog-terrain** – <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/examples/sky-fog-terrain/>

---

### Bottom line
Replace every `map.setFog(...)` call with a `map.setSky({ … })` (or a `sky` object in your style) **and** make sure a `raster-dem` source is active via `map.setTerrain()`. Adjust the fog-related fields (`fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) to get the same atmospheric haze you had in Mapbox GL JS. Happy mapping!
